WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:02.635 line:0%
-One box...

00:00:02.635 --> 00:00:04.971 line:0%
of transcripts that I have.
-Are you kidding me?

00:00:04.971 --> 00:00:08.475 line:0%
-Like, it's hundreds
of thousands of pages.

00:00:08.475 --> 00:00:10.310 line:0%
I'm trying to ask all my friends

00:00:10.310 --> 00:00:12.345 line:0%
to make these audios
come alive.

00:00:12.345 --> 00:00:14.680 line:0%
And I'm trying to turn it into
kind of, like,

00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:15.848 line:0%
a play with voices.

00:00:15.848 --> 00:00:17.350 line:0%
A community looking back.

00:00:17.350 --> 00:00:19.518 line:0%
And so that's what I'm doing
here with you,

00:00:19.518 --> 00:00:21.287 line:0%
and Sammy Rockwell's gonna read

00:00:21.287 --> 00:00:23.122 line:0%
the director
of "Cool Hand Luke."

00:00:23.122 --> 00:00:25.792 line:0%
Laura Linney is gonna do
Joanne Woodward.

00:00:25.792 --> 00:00:28.962 line:0%
Zoe Kazan is gonna play
Paul's first wife.

00:00:28.962 --> 00:00:30.963 line:0%
Karen Allen is playing
Joanne's stepmother.

00:00:30.963 --> 00:00:34.267 line:0%
Josh Hamilton is gonna read
the director of "The Sting."

00:00:34.267 --> 00:00:36.469 line:0%
Vincent D'Onofrio is gonna do
John Huston.

00:00:36.469 --> 00:00:39.672 line:0%
George Clooney agreed to read
Paul.

00:00:39.672 --> 00:00:43.309
So, we're having fun

00:00:43.309 --> 00:00:46.546
kind of revisiting
the generation before us.

00:00:46.546 --> 00:00:47.880
-Welcome back to "Late Night."

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:49.215
We're here with Ethan Hawke,
everybody.

00:00:49.215 --> 00:00:51.884
That was a clip from
"The Last Movie Stars."

00:00:51.884 --> 00:00:57.723
So, one, you're making this
during a pandemic,

00:00:57.723 --> 00:00:59.892
but that's not the only reason

00:00:59.892 --> 00:01:03.896
you reach out to your group
of friends who are actors.

00:01:03.896 --> 00:01:05.898
You had another reason
that you asked them all

00:01:05.898 --> 00:01:08.068
to do voice-over work in this.

00:01:08.068 --> 00:01:10.736
-Well, the family had
an amazing thing.

00:01:10.736 --> 00:01:13.406
It was this -- you see it
in that clip right there.

00:01:13.406 --> 00:01:16.075
They had transcriptions
of interviews

00:01:16.075 --> 00:01:18.578
that Paul had done
to write his own memoir.

00:01:18.578 --> 00:01:20.412
He had had his best friend
interview

00:01:20.412 --> 00:01:23.916
all his closest friends,
nannies, co-workers,

00:01:23.916 --> 00:01:27.420
best friends, everybody
that knew them well.

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:29.255
But he never wrote the memoir.

00:01:29.255 --> 00:01:30.589
So, these interviews
were sitting there

00:01:30.589 --> 00:01:32.592
'cause he got bored
with the whole idea once

00:01:32.592 --> 00:01:34.594
and just set them on fire,
destroyed them,

00:01:34.594 --> 00:01:35.928
got rid of them.

00:01:35.928 --> 00:01:37.763
But the transcripts
still exist.

00:01:37.763 --> 00:01:40.099
But I was so frustrated
when I took this job

00:01:40.099 --> 00:01:42.135
and I was like,
"If I had the audiotapes,

00:01:42.135 --> 00:01:44.637
I could make a really great
film, but I don't."

00:01:44.637 --> 00:01:46.639
And then I had the idea --
well, I am an actor,

00:01:46.639 --> 00:01:49.309
and it is an international
pandemic,

00:01:49.309 --> 00:01:50.976
and nobody's doing anything,

00:01:50.976 --> 00:01:54.146
and so I'll just reenact
these interviews.

00:01:54.146 --> 00:01:55.814
And then I'll have
the audiotapes,

00:01:55.814 --> 00:01:57.817
of which I could
cut them together.

00:01:57.817 --> 00:01:59.486
So, you see, that's what
I'm talking about there

00:01:59.486 --> 00:02:01.987
in that video,
and it ended up being

00:02:01.987 --> 00:02:05.658
really fun because the movie
is a meditation on acting

00:02:05.658 --> 00:02:09.328
and I'm using actors to guide
you through that meditation.

00:02:09.328 --> 00:02:11.330
-And it's really nice
and natural.

00:02:11.330 --> 00:02:12.831
Like I was saying
to you backstage,

00:02:12.831 --> 00:02:16.235
like, at first, it's, "Oh, this
is such a strange construct."

00:02:16.235 --> 00:02:17.937
But then you immediately
become...

00:02:17.937 --> 00:02:19.305
I don't know, it's really...

00:02:19.305 --> 00:02:21.608
You know, when George Clooney
is reading Paul Newman,

00:02:21.608 --> 00:02:22.942
it just seems to make
perfect sense.

00:02:22.942 --> 00:02:25.110
-Just starts to flow.
I remember...

00:02:25.110 --> 00:02:27.113
Scorsese is exec producer
on this

00:02:27.113 --> 00:02:28.614
and he was giving me notes
and he was like,

00:02:28.614 --> 00:02:30.950
"I started watching this,
and for about five minutes,

00:02:30.950 --> 00:02:32.118
I thought you lost your mind."

00:02:32.118 --> 00:02:33.620
[ Laughter ]

00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:35.955
"I don't want to see another
Zoom call as long as I live."

00:02:35.955 --> 00:02:37.924
-Yeah.
-"And then I thought,

00:02:37.924 --> 00:02:39.592
'Wait, I think
this is working.'"

00:02:39.592 --> 00:02:42.094
And I was like, "Yeah, yeah.
Alright, we got it. We got it."

00:02:42.094 --> 00:02:44.264
-It made me really happy
when Scorsese showed up

00:02:44.264 --> 00:02:46.432
because the first five minutes,
I thought,

00:02:46.432 --> 00:02:48.768
"God, no actor knows how
to light their own Zoom,"

00:02:48.768 --> 00:02:50.603
and even Scorsese
looks bad on Zoom.

00:02:50.603 --> 00:02:52.772
[ Laughter ]
-He looks better than most.

00:02:52.772 --> 00:02:54.607
-He looks better than most,
but it's not great.

00:02:54.607 --> 00:02:55.941
Yeah.

00:02:55.941 --> 00:02:59.111
You met Paul Newman
exactly one time.

00:02:59.111 --> 00:03:01.113
-One time.
-And with kind of high stakes,

00:03:01.113 --> 00:03:02.615
I will say.
-Very high stakes.

00:03:02.615 --> 00:03:04.450
I was doing
a Tennessee Williams play.

00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:06.952
The play actually had been
written for Paul Newman

00:03:06.952 --> 00:03:08.454
back in the '50s.

00:03:08.454 --> 00:03:11.624
It's called "Camino Real," and
he and Joanne came to see it.

00:03:11.624 --> 00:03:12.959
And I remember
I was really
sitting

00:03:12.959 --> 00:03:15.395
in my dressing room mirror
and they...

00:03:15.395 --> 00:03:18.564
[ Laughter ]

00:03:18.564 --> 00:03:21.067
There's, like, a gremlin
down there grabbing my arm.

00:03:21.067 --> 00:03:22.402
[ Laughter ]

00:03:22.402 --> 00:03:25.104
They were standing behind me

00:03:25.104 --> 00:03:26.773
and I couldn't believe,
there they were,

00:03:26.773 --> 00:03:28.841
and he was talking about
doing "Sweet Bird of Youth"

00:03:28.841 --> 00:03:30.176
and he'd been in
the original production.

00:03:30.176 --> 00:03:32.511
And I'm just staring at them.

00:03:32.511 --> 00:03:34.680
And he said the first time
he met Tennessee,

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:36.182
he said, you know,
"Nice to meet you,"

00:03:36.182 --> 00:03:39.284
and Tennessee said,
"Oh, yeah, nice to meet you."

00:03:39.284 --> 00:03:41.454
And Paul said, "Well, actually,
we have met before."

00:03:41.454 --> 00:03:43.789
Tennessee said, "Oh, no,
I would remember."

00:03:43.789 --> 00:03:45.258
And he said, "Well, no,
I auditioned for you

00:03:45.258 --> 00:03:46.759
several times,"
And Tennessee said,

00:03:46.759 --> 00:03:48.594
"Mm. Turn around."

00:03:48.594 --> 00:03:50.863
And he looked at his butt
and went, "Oh, yes!

00:03:50.863 --> 00:03:52.198
Now I remember you."

00:03:52.198 --> 00:03:54.199
[ Laughter ]

00:03:54.199 --> 00:03:56.235
But, see, you have to imagine
Paul Newman was telling me

00:03:56.235 --> 00:03:58.070
that story, right behind me.
-Yeah.

00:03:58.070 --> 00:03:59.738
-And I often thought about it

00:03:59.738 --> 00:04:01.573
as I was cutting
the documentary.

00:04:01.573 --> 00:04:04.743
What would he think if he knew
that his kids gave me,

00:04:04.743 --> 00:04:07.079
you know, the responsibility
of telling his story?

00:04:07.079 --> 00:04:08.581
He'd be like, "That punk kid?"

00:04:08.581 --> 00:04:11.050
-Well, I think if he saw
the actual finished product,

00:04:11.050 --> 00:04:12.384
he'd be pretty happy about it.
-I hope so.

00:04:12.384 --> 00:04:13.385
-Yeah.
-I really hope so.

00:04:13.385 --> 00:04:14.887
-Hey, man, congratulations
on this.

00:04:14.887 --> 00:04:16.722
It's just always such a delight
to have you here,

00:04:16.722 --> 00:04:18.891
and so much so that we tried
to trap you on your chair.

00:04:18.891 --> 00:04:19.892
-Yeah, I think
I'll be stuck here.

00:04:19.892 --> 00:04:21.560
I'll be here
for the next person.

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:22.895
-We'll switch the seats.

00:04:22.895 --> 00:04:23.896
Thanks, buddy.
Great to see you.

00:04:23.896 --> 00:04:25.197
Ethan Hawke!

00:04:25.197 --> 00:04:27.567
"The Last Movie Stars"
is streaming now on HBO Max.

